WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.858
A camel lives in the hot desert. A
polar bear lives in the cold Arctic.

2
00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:12.608
Each is suited to its home.
Could it live anywhere else?

3
00:00:13.108 --> 00:00:17.194
A habitat is where an animal
lives in the wild. Here are four.

4
00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:25.216
A camel in the desert, a polar bear in the Arctic.
A monkey in the rainforest, a whale in the ocean.

5
00:00:25.436 --> 00:00:29.755
Its features suit its habitat.
A feature is a part of its body.

6
00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:33.690
A camel has a hump of fat,
and wide feet for the sand.

7
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:38.740
Long lashes keep sand out of its
eyes. Every feature suits the desert.

8
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.601
A polar bear has thick fur over a layer of
fat. So the Arctic cold does not reach it.

9
00:00:46.101 --> 00:00:49.584
Put a polar bear in the desert,
and its fur would cook it.

10
00:00:49.804 --> 00:00:53.798
Put a fish in the desert, and its
gills have no water to breathe.

11
00:00:54.018 --> 00:00:57.315
The features that suit one
place are wrong for another.

12
00:00:57.815 --> 00:01:02.923
Can a living thing survive outside its
habitat? Look it up on a fact card.

13
00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:08.484
The card says yes: food, water,
the right temperature and shelter.

14
00:01:08.704 --> 00:01:13.302
A goldfish lives in a bowl: a person
feeds it and keeps the water clean.

15
00:01:13.522 --> 00:01:18.584
A rainforest plant lives on a windowsill,
if a person waters it and keeps it warm.

16
00:01:18.804 --> 00:01:24.330
Camels taken to Australia survived.
Its hot, dry centre is like their home.

17
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.547
Give it what it needs, and it survives.

18
00:01:27.047 --> 00:01:32.387
A scientific question is one you can
investigate, by observing, counting or testing.

19
00:01:32.607 --> 00:01:36.044
Are woodlice happy? You
cannot go and check that.

20
00:01:36.264 --> 00:01:41.187
Are more woodlice found in damp places
than dry ones? You can count them.

21
00:01:41.407 --> 00:01:46.608
Count under a damp patch and a dry
patch, the same size, at the same time.

22
00:01:46.828 --> 00:01:50.264
Only the dampness is
different. That is a fair test.

23
00:01:50.764 --> 00:01:57.080
Science and technology are all over your
area. Technology is what people make and use.

24
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:05.149
Think of roads, streetlights, recycling centres
and park ponds. Each changes habitats nearby.

25
00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:09.966
A new road brings shops and jobs.
But it cuts a habitat in two.

26
00:02:10.186 --> 00:02:14.505
A pond dug in a park is a new
habitat, for frogs and dragonflies.

27
00:02:15.005 --> 00:02:18.581
Most technology helps some
living things, and harms others.

28
00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:24.699
Streetlights make streets safer at night.
But moths fly round them until they drop.

29
00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:27.798
Science lets you see
both sides, and decide.

30
00:02:28.298 --> 00:02:31.642
Its features suit its habitat,
wrong for another place.

31
00:02:31.862 --> 00:02:35.345
A fact card said it can
survive, if given what it needs.

32
00:02:35.565 --> 00:02:38.165
A good question is one
you can go and check.

33
00:02:38.385 --> 00:02:43.262
Technology near you helps some,
harms others. Weigh up both sides.
